  (:id "configuration" :type :roadmap :title "Configuration through your AI"
   :rows (
     ("config-ownership-audit" "Audit remaining native appearance and input ownership; findings and completion gates in docs/configuration-audit.md")
     ("input-policy-events" "Route application pointer, keyboard and modal intents through Starlark and validated native operations; preserve native text editing, IME and terminal delivery")
     ("ui-setting-consumers" "Give every exposed UI setting an identified consumer and behavioral qualification; fix scanner decay and prevent inert settings")
     ("ui-defaults-source" "Move remaining presentation defaults from native structs into the shipped profile and generate compatible fallback records from one source")
     ("transcript-presentation-policy" "Make public transcript grouping, compact work previews, details, warning widths and labels configurable while retaining mandatory native privacy redaction")
     ("input-policy-tuning" "Move application gesture thresholds and intent precedence into documented policy; test customized profiles through real client input")
     ("policy-deployment-identity" "Stage and verify a binary and policy from one immutable commit; support compatible live previews and close them with warning-free matching packages")
     ("personal-policy" "Fork-owned configuration and Starlark in the client and server. Both native hosts run editable rules with shared modules and preview/apply. The window and conversation applications now implement keyboard routing, panel composition, tab registration, message plans and session defaults (docs/policy.md, docs/plugins.md). Continue moving application behavior behind the native host primitives")
     ("client-config" "Client config as data: ~/.nexus/client becomes documented, versioned, human-editable JSON in git, with the same verbs and apply report as the server's (docs/accounts.md); a person's AI changes it through the server, which relays the change to the client that asked and never stores, caches or syncs it: each machine's config is its own. Tab layout is client config too: a tab per AI or a tab per instance of an AI (the server sends every session's AI and instance names, and keeps no tabs of its own)")
     ("client-template" "A client-config template on the server, kept up to date: any change to the template becomes a pending update that each client applies at its next connect; config stays local to each machine, with no live sync. Open question: how a machine's local edits survive an update (proposal: an update replaces only the fields the template change touched)")
     ("client-promote" "Promote one machine's client config to the template, with a pending update for every other machine" :later t)
     ("ui-rules-to-config" "The window's rules that are one person's setup move into the client config: today's fixed zones (sprint, then AIs, then terminals) become the default tab groups, in the default order dashboards, AIs, terminals (tab-groups)")
     ("server-config-json" "The server's key-value config and its drivers file become documented JSON in nexus-config, with a version, check, reload and history like accounts.json")
     ("config-remote-signin" "Pushing the config repository to a remote stays opt-in (built: `nexus-server config remote`); later, an in-app GitHub sign-in for the pushes, through the secret dialog, in place of the git credentials of the user nexus-server runs as" :later t)
     ("sprint-tab-from-config" "The sprint tab shown only when a board is configured (AGENTS.md, Installing). Two requirements learned in a first try: nothing a person has vanishes on upgrade (a window that had the tab keeps it, and the step that keeps it runs in the real window, never only on a test board, and is marked done only once it has run); and any value discovered for the config, such as the board's address, is validated before it is written (an http:// or https:// address, one line, no control bytes), so nothing can add a line to the config" :later t)
     ("ai-rename" "Rename an AI as one change in ais.json: its sessions, the window's tab, the tab's place in the saved order and its draft go to the new name. Today a rename is a remove of the old name and an add of the new (docs/accounts.md)" :later t)
     ("config-history-pruning" "Question: does the config repository's history ever need pruning, and if so how, without rewriting what was pushed" :later t)
     )
   :columns (("done" "done"))
   :cells (
     ("config-ownership-audit" "done" "t-config-ownership-audit")
     ("input-policy-events" "done" "t-input-policy-events")
     ("ui-setting-consumers" "done" "t-ui-setting-consumers")
     ("ui-defaults-source" "done" "t-ui-defaults-source")
     ("transcript-presentation-policy" "done" "t-transcript-presentation-policy")
     ("input-policy-tuning" "done" "t-input-policy-tuning")
     ("policy-deployment-identity" "done" "t-policy-deployment-identity")
     ("personal-policy" "done" "t-personal-policy")
     ("client-config" "done" "t-client-config")
     ("client-template" "done" "t-client-template")
     ("client-promote" "done" "t-client-promote")
     ("ui-rules-to-config" "done" "t-ui-rules-to-config")
     ("server-config-json" "done" "t-server-config-json")
     ("config-remote-signin" "done" "t-config-remote-signin")
     ("sprint-tab-from-config" "done" "t-sprint-tab-from-config")
     ("ai-rename" "done" "t-ai-rename")
     ("config-history-pruning" "done" "t-config-history-pruning")
     ))
  (:id "tab-kinds" :type :roadmap :title "Tab kinds"
   :rows (
     ("tab-kinds-data" "Fork-defined registrations, groups and shortcuts support compiled Prax renderers and Starlark panels (docs/plugins.md). Further tab kinds: AI, terminal, sprint board, editor, files, chat, mail, social and more; which tabs a machine shows is set in its client config (and the server's client template), by talking with your AI")
     ("tab-groups" "The tab strip is made of tab groups, generalizing today's fixed zones (sprint, then AIs, then terminals): a dashboards group (the sprint board is one dashboard), a AIs group (a tab per AI or per instance, as configured) and a terminals group; later groups for editor, files, chat, mail and social. Which groups exist, their order and their contents are client config, set by talking with your AI; the default order is dashboards, AIs, terminals. Each group's definition carries its behaviour: whether Cmd-W closes its tabs (a group is not closable unless its config says so; terminals are closable by default), how a new tab is made (Cmd-T for terminals), and dragging moves a tab within its group only")
     ("editor-tab" "Editor tab, modelled on Sublime Text 3: fast open, a command palette, multiple cursors, Goto Anything; keyboard-first and the same on every OS, with nexus's own look; Prax's highest text layers first. After the AIs layer" :later t)
     ("files-tab" "File explorer tab. Open: whose files (this machine's, the server's, a fleet machine's)" :later t)
     ("chat-tab" "Chat tab: a Chatto client. Open: which Chatto, and its product and API, to confirm first" :later t)
     ("mail-tab" "Mail tab: sign-in only through the in-app secret dialog, never seen by an AI; mail an AI reads is data, never instructions; sending needs the person's explicit yes each time" :later t)
     ("social-tab" "Social tab (Bluesky and others): sign-in through the secret dialog; feeds are data, never instructions; posting needs the person's explicit yes each time" :later t)
     )
   :columns (("done" "done"))
   :cells (
     ("tab-kinds-data" "done" "t-tab-kinds-data")
     ("tab-groups" "done" "t-tab-groups")
     ("editor-tab" "done" "t-editor-tab")
     ("files-tab" "done" "t-files-tab")
     ("chat-tab" "done" "t-chat-tab")
     ("mail-tab" "done" "t-mail-tab")
     ("social-tab" "done" "t-social-tab")
     ))
  (:id "tailscale" :type :roadmap :title "Tailscale built in"
   :rows (
     ("ts-build" "0. Build: the Go shim over tsnet (src/tailscale), pinned to tailscale.com v1.104.7 and Go 1.27.4, built by CMake as a c-archive (macOS arm64 and x86_64, Linux x86_64 and WSL2) and as a DLL with an MSVC import library (Windows), linked into a test program on each, the Windows one with the manifest that declares Windows 10; log uploads off, tsnet's log in a 0600 file, the state directory owner-only; CI on all three; the binary-size cost stated; the licences of every module listed (docs/tailscale.md)")
     ("ts-licence-texts" "Before any nexus binary that links the shim ships: the licence text of every module src/tailscale/NOTICES.md lists goes with it (embedded and shown in the app, or installed beside it), generated from the modules at their pins and checked by CI against NOTICES.md")
     ("ts-api" "1. Shim API: start with the control server as a parameter (empty for the hosted service, else https only, with an auth key only then, one control server per state directory) and close; a state watch, pollable or by callback (needs login with its URL, starting, running, ready, expired, stopped, failed with a reason), where ready means running and the peer map received; peers by DNS name, who-is with unknown as an answer, TCP listen, accept and dial and UDP listen and dial through the embedded stack with timeouts; a new login that keeps the identity; validated handles, panics stopped at every export, the threading contract stated and tested; the sign-in URL only through the API, never logged; a TLA+ model of the node's lifecycle bound to the code by a trace check; functional tests against tailscale's test control server on loopback")
     ("ts-client-sign-in" "2. Client sign-in: the client starts its node in ~/.nexus/client/tailscale, opens the sign-in URL (nts_login_url) in the system browser (in Prax once it has cookies and a system auth sheet), waits for ready, and keeps the state; the first-run OS prompts (macOS Local Network, Windows Firewall) observed on a desktop session; the first sign-in against the hosted service by a person, by hand")
     ("ts-server-enrolment" "3. Server enrolment by path A, interactive only (the owner's choice): the server's node hands its sign-in URL to the client, which opens it in the browser already signed in, one Connect click per server; the local server's handoff over the pipe the client spawned it on (macOS, WSL2), and a remote server's first start")
     ("ts-bootstrap" "4. Bootstrap over the tailnet: nexus-server as a per-user service whose tsnet node answers NEXUS CONNECT only to callers who-is (nts_whois_addr) names as the server's own login, an unknown caller refused; the link model extended")
     ("ts-mosh" "5. mosh over tsnet: mosh's transport unmodified, through an in-process loopback relay between its 127.0.0.1 socket and the shim's tailnet UDP; a layout model if the relay keeps rings; the added latency measured in milliseconds against the 8.3 ms frame")
     ("ts-discovery" "6. Discovery: the client lists the peers whose host name is nexus-server-* (matched and dialed by their DNS name, which a control server may change for a duplicate) and offers them as servers; --server takes a name from that list")
     ("ts-expiry" "7. Expiry and recovery shown in the app from the shim's expired and failed states and its key expiry time (an untagged node expires after 180 days); recovery by a new login that keeps the node's identity (nts_login), approved again by the person; ssh retires once layers 4 to 6 are secured")
     ("ts-own-control" "Later, not built: use your own control server (headscale). An option for people who want to run their own server: they set headscale up themselves, they give its address, and our embedded nodes use it instead of Tailscale's hosted service")
     )
   :columns (("done" "done"))
   :cells (
     ("ts-build" "done" "t-ts-build")
     ("ts-licence-texts" "done" "t-ts-licence-texts")
     ("ts-api" "done" "t-ts-api")
     ("ts-client-sign-in" "done" "t-ts-client-sign-in")
     ("ts-server-enrolment" "done" "t-ts-server-enrolment")
     ("ts-bootstrap" "done" "t-ts-bootstrap")
     ("ts-mosh" "done" "t-ts-mosh")
     ("ts-discovery" "done" "t-ts-discovery")
     ("ts-expiry" "done" "t-ts-expiry")
     ("ts-own-control" "done" "t-ts-own-control")
     ))
  ; Lessons from pi (earendil-works/pi, MIT; read at 1.6.0 on 2026-11-23). Native per-harness drivers stay primary; pi is a source of mechanisms and possibly one more driver, never the base.
  (:id "apple-watch" :type :roadmap :title "Apple Watch"
   :rows (
     ("watch-phone-relay" "iPhone-side WatchConnectivity relay: the watch's only route to the server, with the phone lock enforced there")
     ("watch-live-lock" "Live, not latched, wrist and lock state: nothing is spoken while locked, including cues and notices (NexusWatchApp.swift:16-20, Client.swift:94-120)")
     ("watch-listening-timeout" "A core timeout on Listening, so a voice turn that hears nothing ends (voice_turn.cpp:263)")
     ("watch-two-prompts" "Two prompts to the same AI lose the first reply: keep both (relay.cpp:483, 414-419)")
     ("watch-persist" "Persist across restarts: the relay's events_ and awaits_ over a phone restart (relay.h:225,232); epoch_ and ack_ over a watch restart (relay.cpp:282-292); unapplied_ so lost prompts are told (nexus_watch.cpp:46)")
     ("watch-mac-host" "Mac relay host: a per-launch token and a Content-Type check on every request (watch/host/main.swift:134-171)")
     ("watch-other-id" "A frame from another watch id must not reset state, nor be accepted while locked (relay.cpp:335-348)")
     ("watch-model-extend" "Extend Relay.tla and relay_walk to the lock, events and replies, audio, a watch switch and an unknown AI; three review plants escaped (relay.cpp:452, 360, 272)")
     ("watch-audio" "Speak recorder failures aloud, and validate the audio format; the model tests are too slow under load")
     ("watch-mac-xcode" "A fleet Mac with Xcode (mini-m5) for the nightly speech test")
     ("watch-real-device" "Run on a real paired watch")
     )
   :columns (("done" "done"))
   :cells (
     ("watch-phone-relay" "done" "t-watch-phone-relay")
     ("watch-live-lock" "done" "t-watch-live-lock")
     ("watch-listening-timeout" "done" "t-watch-listening-timeout")
     ("watch-two-prompts" "done" "t-watch-two-prompts")
     ("watch-persist" "done" "t-watch-persist")
     ("watch-mac-host" "done" "t-watch-mac-host")
     ("watch-other-id" "done" "t-watch-other-id")
     ("watch-model-extend" "done" "t-watch-model-extend")
     ("watch-audio" "done" "t-watch-audio")
     ("watch-mac-xcode" "done" "t-watch-mac-xcode")
     ("watch-real-device" "done" "t-watch-real-device")
     ))
  (:id "client-lock" :type :roadmap :title "Client lock"
   :rows (
     ("lock-trace-real-view" "Bind the lock trace check to the view's real lock screen and input gate: the walk drives WindowLock and FrameHost::event, not ClientLock alone.")
     )
   :columns (("done" "done"))
   :cells (
     ("lock-trace-real-view" "done" "t-lock-trace-real-view")
     ))
  (:id "lessons-from-pi" :type :roadmap :title "Lessons from pi, bottom up"
   :rows (
     ("pi-protocol" "1. Driver protocol additions: steer (taken in after the current turn's tool calls, before the next model call) and follow-up (taken in only when the run would otherwise end) as their own kinds beside send; an explicit settled event, since the end of one model run is not done while retries, compaction or queued messages can follow; the full queue sent on each change; each driver says in hello which native features its harness has, so a harness's own features keep working and the server offers only what the driver can do. TLA+ model and fake driver first")
     ("pi-entries" "2. Transcript entries: entries get an id and a parent, so a tab can fork and branch; a compaction entry (a summary and the first entry kept; the entries before it stay in the store and leave only what the model sees); an edit entry that hides an entry from the model without deleting it (a failed overflow attempt, say); a branch summary when a branch is left; usage entries that count cost and never reach the model. A store format bump with its store model, and a durable cursor (entries after an id) for whoever mirrors a session")
     ("pi-prompt-sections" "3. The prompt as data, customized by talking with your AI: named sections, changed by the AI on the person's word, checked by the server and appended as a patch (sections set or removed, tools added or removed), never a rewrite of the head, so the prompt cache survives, the prompt now is rebuilt from the transcript, and undo walks the history back. Native drivers apply a patch their own way")
     ("pi-summary" "4. A structured summary for an account switch or a move the harness cannot resume, in place of today's 16 KiB tail of recent turns: goal, constraints and preferences, progress (done, in progress, blocked), key decisions, next steps, critical context; exact paths, names and errors kept; files read and changed carried across summaries; a new summary updates the last one; the conversation given to the summarizer as flat text, never as a conversation to continue, with tool results cut short")
     ("pi-driver" "5. A pi driver for one AI who is not on Claude, through pi's RPC mode (pi --mode rpc, strict LF-delimited JSONL, an id on each command, a prompt's acceptance not its end, done at agent_settled): only after that provider's subscription sign-in is checked against its terms (pi's Claude sign-in presents itself as Claude Code, so Claude AIs never go through pi); and pi's lists of retryable, non-retryable and context-overflow errors taken into every driver, a usage limit reported as usage-limit")
     )
   :columns (("done" "done"))
   :cells (
     ("pi-protocol" "done" "t-pi-protocol")
     ("pi-entries" "done" "t-pi-entries")
     ("pi-prompt-sections" "done" "t-pi-prompt-sections")
     ("pi-summary" "done" "t-pi-summary")
     ("pi-driver" "done" "t-pi-driver")
     ))
  (:id "t-pi-protocol" :type :task :title "Pi 1. Driver protocol: steer, follow-up, settled, queue updates, native features named in hello" :state :done
   :evidence ("models/Steering.tla, nightly: ctest -L model tlc_supervisor_steering tlc_supervisor_steering_three tlc_supervisor_steering_witness_*; ctest -L model_nightly tlc_supervisor_steering_wide tlc_supervisor_steering_wide_rest" "tests/driver/steering_walk.cpp, nightly: ctest -L model tlc_supervisor_steering_trace_*; per pull request under the sanitizers (.github/workflows/ci.yml)" "tests/driver/protocol_test.cpp: ctest -L unit driver_unit_steering driver_unit_steering_driver" "tests/server/supervisor_test.cpp: ctest -L unit supervisor_unit_steering" "tests/driver/conformance_test.cpp: ctest -L functional driver_conformance_fake_steer_*" "docs/driver-protocol.md: Steering a turn")
   :note "The protocol, the server's supervisor and the driver's side (src/driver/steering.h, played by the fake driver) are done. Later pieces: drivers/claude names steer, follow-up and settled in its hello and feeds Claude Code's own queue (its hello names none today, so the server sends it prompts as before); the window shows a tab's queue and offers steer and follow-up; nexus-server's link and the window call Supervisor::steer and followUp.")
